# Line endings are load-bearing here, and this repo is authored on Windows
# (core.autocrlf=true). Without a rule, a checkout writes CRLF into every text file,
# and two separate things break on an invisible byte:
#
#  1. tools/launch-server.sh fails on Linux and macOS with "bad interpreter: no such
#     file or directory" — a stranger's clone breaking before it runs anything.
#
#  2. THE GOLD IS OFFSETS-ONLY. gold-offsets.mjs publishes spans as character offsets
#     into corpus/ and raw/ documents rather than as verbatim text, because the source
#     corpora are not ours to redistribute. An offset counts bytes from the start of the
#     file, so one extra CR per line shifts every span after line 1. On this machine the
#     documents are the output of build-corpus.mjs and are LF, which is why nothing has
#     ever failed here; on a fresh Windows CLONE they arrive CRLF and the whole gold
#     instrument silently scores against the wrong spans. Nothing in the repo would
#     notice, which is what makes it worth a rule rather than a note.
#
# The rule: everything is LF on checkout everywhere, batch files excepted because cmd.exe
# needs CRLF, and .sh stays explicit so the reason above survives a rewrite of this file.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Binary and byte-exact assets: never touch these. `text=auto` above would sniff most of
# them correctly, but sniffing is a heuristic and the cost of one wrong guess is a .docx
# fixture that no longer opens or a font that renders as squares, discovered by a user
# rather than by us. 76 files here are binary; the extensions are named rather than
# inferred. .bcmap and .pfb come with pdfjs-dist and are read byte-for-byte by the PDF
# reader; .traineddata is the OCR model.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.docx binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.pfb  binary
*.bcmap binary
*.gguf binary
*.traineddata binary
*.zip  binary

